            "The least restrictive level is configural invariance, which addresses the question of whether the factor structure of the items is the same in the two groups.",
            "The second level is metric invariance, which asks whether the factor loadings are the same between groups that have similar configural factor structures.",
            "The third level is scalar invariance, which checks whether the item intercepts are the same in the two groups after adjusting for the level of the latent variable of interest.",
            "The final level is strict invariance, which asks whether the error variances of items are the same in the two groups, assuming scalar invariance.",
            "To assess configural invariance, one usually carries out factor analyses of each group separately, and reasonable fit in both groups is consistent with configural invariance.",
